    ["“知识与智慧之神”向你投来注视。"] = "The God of Knowledge and Wisdom is casting his gaze upon you.",
    ["　　……\n　　一个贵族夫人遇到了凶恶的劫匪。\n　　玫瑰夫人不禁哀叹自己生命的短暂与脆弱，心中盛满了悲伤。\n　　……\n　　……\n　　没有想到，英雄从天而降，拯救了陷入泥沼的玫瑰。\n　　……\n　　……\n　　这一刻，她忘记了方才的危险与过往无趣的生活，她仿佛看到了一本世界上最有趣的冒险小说，而自己竟然就是故事书的女主人公！\n　　……"] = "... \n A noble lady encountered a vicious robber. \n Lady Rose could not help but lament the brevity and fragility of her life, her heart filled with sadness. \n ... \n ... \n Unexpectedly, a hero descended from the sky and saved the rose that had fallen into the mire. \n ... \n ... \n At this moment, she forgot the danger just now and her boring life in the past; she felt as if she had seen the most interesting adventure novel in the world, and she was actually the heroine of the storybook! \n ...",
    ["　　……任何两个整数，若它们除以某个正整数的余数相等，则称它们关于该模数同余。同余理论是数论的基石，然而在更广阔的代数数域中，唯一分解律并不总是成立。\n　　我们不得不引入理想数的概念来弥补这一缺陷。\n　　这一缺陷最初只是作为反例被注意到——在某些二次域中，同一个整数可以被分解为不同的素因子乘积，分解的唯一性荡然无存。这对整个算术大厦构成了根本性的动摇。"] = " ...Any two integers, if their remainders when divided by a certain positive integer are equal, are said to be congruent with respect to that modulus. Congruence theory is the cornerstone of number theory, yet in the broader algebraic number field, the law of unique factorization does not always hold. \n We have to introduce the concept of ideal numbers to make up for this defect. \n This defect was initially noticed only as a counterexample—in some quadratic fields, the same integer can be decomposed into products of different prime factors, and the uniqueness of decomposition vanishes. This constitutes a fundamental shaking of the entire arithmetic edifice.",
    ["　　在霍纳奇斯山脉下有一处终年被银色雾气包裹的森林，里面住着各自各样的珍稀动物。其中，有一头叫做邓恩的麋鹿住在森林的最深处，它有着云朵般柔软的灰色皮毛和比橡木还要坚实的巨大鹿角，走路时的蹄声沉稳地如同森林的呼吸。它是这座森林的守护者，拥有洞察森林每一个角落的力量，但这份力量也伴随着诅咒——每年第一场雪落下的时候，它就会开始遗忘。\n\n　　先是刚刚的对话，然后是昨天帮助过的动物，最后连自己的名字都如同飘落的雪花，落在地上了无痕迹。他只好把重要的事情都刻下来，做成木牌挂在鹿角上提醒自己。\n\n　　有一只叫戴莉的知更鸟住在森林的东边，雾气更加浓郁的地方。它有着绯红色的羽毛，它可以用歌声编织出金色的旋律，为迷路的生灵指引方向。但这里藏着它的秘密：每编织一段完整的旋律，它就会掉落一根羽毛，掉落的羽毛不会再生长。"] = "　　At the foot of the Hornacis Mountain Range, there is a forest covered in silver mist all year round, where various rare animals live. Among them, a reindeer named Dunn lives in the deepest part of the forest. It has gray fur as soft as clouds and huge antlers stronger than oak, and its hoofbeats when walking are as steady as the forest's breathing. It is the guardian of this forest and possesses the power to gain insight into every corner of the forest, but this power comes with a curse—every year when the first snow falls, it begins to forget. \n\n　　First, it's the conversation just now, then the animals it helped yesterday, and finally even its own name falls to the ground like falling snowflakes, leaving no trace. It has to carve important things onto wooden boards and hang them on its antlers to remind itself. \n\n　　There is a robin named Daly living in the east of the forest, where the mist is thicker. It has crimson feathers, and it can weave golden melodies with its singing to guide lost creatures. But here lies its secret: every time it weaves a complete melody, it loses a feather, and the lost feathers will not grow back.",
